1. Choosing Stocks and Cryptocurrencies
1.1 Stocks
As a bondholder one enjoys certain privileges and one main drawback.

A bondholder is contractually guaranteed a fixed flow of coupon payments at specified intervals of times. As long as the company does not default, these payments continue until maturity, when the final coupon and principal are repaid together.
In case of a default, bondholders get paid before equity holders.
Aside from a situation of default, the amount of the principal returned is equal to the value of the investment made.
These payments are fixed and if the companies did very well in the market, a bondholder would not receive any gains from that upside. Indeed, this is one of the drawbacks of bonds.
A different type of security, stocks, is aimed at removing the drawback described immediately above. In fact, the ability to participate specifically in a company's upside is the strength of stocks.

Stocks are securities that represent a fractional ownership in a corporation: a stockholder, or shareholder, actually owns a fraction of the company. Stocks used to be physical certificates that entitled the holder to both a proportional amount of ownership and a share of the company's profits. Today, stock ownership is managed digitally. In the same manner, stock ownership allows the holder to vote on decisions about the management of the company. In reality, most stockholders own such a tiny portion of shares that they have no influence on the management. Collectively, the stockholders tend to vote for a board of directors. In turn, this board of directors selects a CEO and other senior managers, who in turn decide on all staffing decisions among other things.

Another name for stocks is equities. This is because stockholders are entitled to the equity ownership of the company. We will see more of this in a later module, but for now, think of equity as the difference between assets and liabilities. This is a simple consequence of the Fundamental Equation of Accounting, i.e.:

