2. Cryptocurrency
Many students who take this course have only a vague notion of cryptocurrencies. Hence, a quick introduction is beneficial. Cryptocurrencies are based on blockchain technology. As a technique, blockchain goes back to the early 1990s, more precisely to the work of Haber and Stornetta in 1991. Then, in 2002, Back introduced the idea of proof of work, and finally, in 2008, Satoshi Nakamoto used these ideas to introduce Bitcoin.

We surely do not have the time and space for an accurate treatment of cryptocurrencies, which would require hundreds of pages, but it is important to understand the main ideas behind the concept.

Those who are familiar with accounting are certainly aware of what a ledger is. Nevertheless, we give the definition here as well for the benefit of those students who have never been exposed to accounting. A ledger is a book, possibly a digital book, that contains bookkeeping entries, and it is used to summarize information for all of a company's financial transactions over a pre-specified period. A ledger then serves as a master document.

A blockchain is a shared and immutable ledger that facilitates the process of recording transactions and makes it possible to track assets in a business. A business relies on this information to function, and the faster and more accurate the information is, the better. In its essence, a blockchain network can track orders, payments, etc., and all members within the company share a single view of the actual business, can see all the details, have confidence in what the company is doing, know what the company does well or not so well, and gain a sense of what opportunities exist. We could say that a blockchain is a distributed ledger technology or a distributed database.

Traditionally, the database/ledger is entrusted and controlled by a centralized figure or organization. When Nakamoto introduced Bitcoin, he created a single distributed database that is accessible to everyone, i.e., anyone is able to see the balances and enter and submit new transactions at any time, but the ledger is not controlled by anyone specifically and there is no need for permission to access or use it. In this sense, the ledger is maintained on a decentralized basis. In centralized organizations, different parties send their information to a central database, then someone checks that everything is submitted properly before the information is entered into the official database. On the other hand, in a decentralized situation without a central control center, if everyone involved can agree on the status of the database at any time, all the databases can be synced, thereby saving time and usually reducing cost as well.

