Clearly, if the idea of a permissionless ledger is to exist and function, there are a number of technical challenges that need to be solved. Think of this: essentially, in a decentralized world, we have several copies of the same database floating around on thousands or millions of different machines and, since no one is in charge, we have to make sure that all copies are identical, are updated together, and reflect real transactions (as opposed to fraudulent ones). The solution to these problems is what gave us blockchains:

All participants (nodes) have a copy of the same ledger;
Once a ledger is accepted as the true ledger, it cannot be changed anymore and all transactions that are validated are irreversible;
Transactions must be time-stamped to avoid the possibility of adding the same transaction several times;
All nodes agree on the validity of each block and the blockchain as the whole of the individual blocks;
The participants remain anonymous;
All transactions/records are encrypted; and
Blockchains are programmable. This is a bit difficult to understand without any previous knowledge of the field, but it is a necessary feature to be able to use smart contracts.
Is a blockchain secure? The question is legitimate because, without trust, people would never share valuable information with people they do not know and/or do not trust. Blockchains store data using sophisticated math and complex innovative software rules that are difficult for an attacker to penetrate and modify. However, this does not mean that they are completely unbreakable.

Bitcoin's blockchain contains the shared history of every Bitcoin transaction ever made just like an accounting ledger. Multiple copies of this ledger are stored on a network of computers and these computers are called nodes. Each time someone submits a transaction to the ledger, the computers/nodes check that the transaction is valid (for instance, they check that whoever used bitcoins to pay for the transaction actually had those bitcoins to spend). The valid transactions become blocks, and the blocks are then added to a chain that already contains previous blocks. These nodes have owners, and the owners of these nodes are called miners. When miners successfully add new blocks to a chain, they earn a reward (bitcoins). Thus, we now have a sense of how a cryptocurrency like Bitcoin is created.

Now that we understand what a block is, we can describe the forms of protection that make the blockchain secure:

each block has a cryptographic fingerprint (called hash);
a consensus protocol that is the process by which the nodes in a network agree on a shared history.
Initially, the fingerprint requires a lot of computing time and energy to generate. It is used as proof that the miner who added the block to the blockchain did the computational work in order to earn the bitcoin reward (proof-of-work protocol). Then, the hash becomes a seal, and altering the block would require generating a new hash. However, verifying that a hash matches its block is easy. Once the nodes have verified the hash, they update their copies of the blockchain by adding the new block. This is what we referred to earlier as the consensus protocol. Finally, the hashes also serve as the links in the blockchain in the sense that each block includes the previous block's unique hash. This is important because if we wanted to change an entry in the ledger later, we would have to calculate a new hash for that block and for all the blocks that come after it. And there's more: we would have to do it faster than the other blocks can add new blocks to the chain, which requires computers that are more powerful than those used at the other nodes of the network combined! This gives us a chance, but not a guarantee, of being able to pull off that trick; otherwise, there would be conflicts among some of the nodes, and the alterations would be rejected.

We also mentioned smart contracts. Ethereum, a decentralized blockchain introduced in 2015 by Vitalik Buterin, is an example of a platform that allows for the running of computer programs called smart contracts. Hence, the code of the programs can create and transform data or tokens on top of the blockchain of which it is part. Smart contracts control assets and data and define transactions between assets, data, and network participants. In this sense, they are quite powerful, as a user could encode rules to define any type of transaction, which could create new and scarce assets for special purposes. Ethereum allows anyone to deploy permanent and immutable applications on it. This can be seen as one of the reasons for decentralized finance (DeFi) to exist. Furthermore, on Ethereum, users are allowed to interact not just with other users but even with smart contracts. Ethereum uses Ether as the native currency for the platform. We can't get too deep into this topic, but smart contracts, called ERC (Ethereum Request for Comment), can establish certain rules. For instance, ERC-20 is a fungible token, and we could use this smart contract to establish that a 1 EUR bill has the same value of another 1 EUR bill. ERC-721 is a smart contract that establishes that each token is associated with a particular asset and thus not fungible. While Bitcoin uses a proof-of-work consensus mechanism, Ethereum uses a proof-of-stake mechanism instead.

The software applications we mentioned earlier live on a decentralized smart contract platform. For this reason, they are called decentralized applications and they are permissionless, i.e., anyone can use them and there is no central body that controls them. Instead, there is a collectively owned organization called DAO (Decentralized Autonomous Organization) with its rules of operation encoded in smart contracts and determining who can execute what behavior to upgrade. Ethereum wallets are applications that give individuals control over their accounts. They contain everything one needs to prove one's identity and they can handle one's assets. Individuals can sign in, check their balances, send transactions, etc. Essentially, wallets are tools for interacting with Ethereum accounts. Any user with any amount of ETH can help make the networks secure and earn rewards in the process in exchange for running software that properly batches transactions into new blocks and checks the work of other validators.

The Ethereum blockchain is only aware of what happens on the Ethereum blockchain. But what if the information needed is outside of such a blockchain? This is where an oracle solves the problem. An oracle is a bridge between the blockchain and the external world, providing a way to bring off-chain data onto the blockchain. Oracles are especially important since many meaningful applications need to use data from around the world. Let's assume, for example, that we have a decentralized application that accepts bets on a soccer match. We have three possible outcomes: Team A wins, Team B wins, and a draw. Let's also assume that this application has gathered a lot of bets that will be distributed among those who selected the correct outcome. The question now is who is responsible for inputting the correct result into the app? It is easy to understand that malicious actors have every motivation to find ways to input the data they want into a decentralized application. To solve this problem, blockchain engineers have created various types of oracles such as centralized, decentralized, consensus-based, and human-based. The choice of each type depends on the tradeoff between safety, ease of use, simplicity, and cost of operation.

In our quick introduction, we talked about Bitcoin and Ethereum, albeit briefly, to ensure that every student has at least a modicum of understanding of cryptocurrencies. But there are many more besides Bitcoin (BTC) and Ether (ETH): Tether, Solana, Litecoin, Bitcoin Cash, BNB, Dogecoin, and many, many more. Then, there are online exchanges, for instance Coinbase, where one can buy major cryptocurrencies, and you can use bitcoins to buy a computer or ethers to play digital games, etc.

Some people look at cryptocurrencies as investments, and they behave like other investments. For instance, it is not unusual for BTC to increase or decrease in by price by  on any given day. Smaller cryptocurrencies can be even more volatile.

Shareholders may find the selection of stocks daunting considering the bewildering number of stock choices in different countries on different exchanges. And, even if we selected a specific company, we may find that its current stock price is too high. So while we did our homework, it's possible that the opportunity to purchase at a good price is long gone. In this case, we could decide to short the stock. However, short selling is very risky because the potential loss is unlimited. The price can increase without bound, causing the loss to increase without bound! Hence, we could decide that we want a different asset class all together, and this is where cryptocurrencies could become appealing to investors.

Cryptocurrencies are digital currencies that have disrupted the financial system. Typically, money was issued by a government, perhaps backed by gold, and served as an exclusive form of legal tender for a specific country or multinational economic association (e.g., the euro in the European Union). Cryptocurrencies transcend borders. They lack central regulating or issuing authorities. Bitcoin originated just as the world was experiencing the Great Recession. Trust in Bitcoin has replaced, to a degree, trust in the prudent, unbiased decision-making authority of governmental and central banks with “objective, rational, incorruptible mathematical calculations” in a public decentralized ledger (Stellinga et al. 7). Indeed, the advantage of cryptocurrencies is that they are using a decentralized system to record the entire history of transactions.

2.1 Cryptocurrency Benefits
Now that we have provided an overview of what cryptocurrency is, let's discuss its benefits. First, cryptocurrencies have extreme returns. You may find a stock that trades aggressively and whose price fluctuates, but it would be much easier to find a cryptocurrency that trades with greater amounts of volatility. If you believe that you have a good way to time markets (you know how to buy low and sell high), then cryptocurrencies might be right for you.

Another advantage is that there is exponential growth. Less than twenty years ago, cryptocurrencies didn't exist. Now, there are at least 10,000 active cryptocurrencies. Today, there are between $1 and $2 trillion (two million million) dollars in several different cryptocurrencies.

The third benefit is that cryptocurrencies trade around the clock: 24 hours a day, 7 days a week. This is because current cryptocurrencies defy borders; that is, they do not physically exist in a location like the Tokyo Stock Exchange that closes during off-business hours in Japan. Human traders go home after a day's work, but the cryptocurrency markets are always open! The ability to trade at any hour means that you can react immediately to news. If there is significant news that impacts the market, you can trade quickly without waiting until the next business day. This can be a big advantage when compared to stocks. For example, suppose you had sold short Peloton stock going into a weekend. Over the weekend, it was announced that there might be a takeover. By the time the stocks opened the following Monday, the stocks increased over 30%. If you were a short seller, you would have lost 30%. Around-the-clock market access would have prevented that, but it is not a feature of stocks like it is for cryptocurrencies.

2.2 Cryptocurrency Disadvantages
There are most certainly disadvantages to cryptocurrencies. It is very difficult to apply the value mechanism that we use for stocks to cryptocurrencies. This is because cryptocurrencies tend not to pay dividends. Therefore, it is hard to understand the value. Indeed, some investors have shunned Bitcoin. One of the world’s most famous investors Warren Buffett said that Bitcoin is “probably rat poison squared” ("Crypto is 'rat poison'") and referred to it as a gambling instrument. In 2021, famous financial practitioner and author Nassim Nicholas Taleb said that Bitcoin is worth zero and fails a hedge (Macheel). JP Morgan CEO Jamie Dimon was quoted as saying that “Cryptocurrency has no intrinsic value…You are basically buying a token” (Helms).

The only way to make money in cryptocurrency is to hold it until some other investor believes it's worth even more. Unlike stocks, which are issued by companies that make products or provide services, cryptocurrencies are an implied value that's recognized by the holders at large. Be sure to distinguish cryptocurrencies from real-world currencies. Real-world currencies are known as foreign exchange or FX. This is where you could trade, for example, using the exchange rate between Great British pound sterling and Japanese yen.

2.3 Bitcoin vs. Bonds
Finally, let's discuss the difference between Bitcoin and bonds. Indeed, these are probably the two ends of the spectrum in terms of return. Risk-free bonds are likely the most conservative investment, in that you are virtually guaranteed your principal back plus a specified amount of interest so long as the issue remains solvent. Bitcoin, on the other hand, has little if any intrinsic value. Again, we emphasize that cryptocurrencies are not from companies that make and issue products and services. Cryptocurrencies are adding value in that they are providing a publicly distributed ledger for a huge number of applications both in and outside of finance. Nevertheless, the value is quite subjective. That being said, Bitcoin has gone from zero to tens of thousands of dollars. Even as these lesson notes are being written, Bitcoin is projected to achieve almost $100,000 per Bitcoin. It would take a stock investment many years to achieve such a high increase, but if you held a bond to maturity, you are effectively earning the yield to maturity. If you paid par for the bond, the yield to maturity would equal the coupon rate. This yield, or interest rate, is a small fraction of the overall principal, so the overall return is not very large. However, in Bitcoin, it is possible to make 50%, 100%, 200%, or even 1,000% or several thousand percent. Keep in mind, though, that what goes up in markets can also come down. It is just as easy if not easier to lose 90% of your money. Consequently, it's possible you could have bought Bitcoin and experienced a huge market correction of 40 or 50%. You can see stories where athletes are paid in Bitcoin. For some, they find that by the time they go to use the funds, they have a considerably smaller amount to withdraw from ("Buy Bitcoin Worldwide").

3. Conclusion
In this lesson, we looked at the basics of stocks and cryptocurrencies, highlighting their advantages and disadvantages. Compared to fixed-income securities, stocks and Bitcoin have more upside potential but also more downside risk. Choosing the right one at the right time is key. In the next lesson, we will examine the factors to consider when selecting stocks and cryptocurrencies.

